#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119634 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119823 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29b27833-e930-5339-89b3-c5651d791dcd" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Two-Dimensional Vector in a Different Basis"}] :question/id #uuid "6a082146-0d63-59dd-9224-a319d73ca59c" :question/math-academy-id "e-4827" :question/problem "Let $\\mathcal{B}= \\left\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2 \\right\\}$ be a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and ![](images/71/7138c7f0cf14978e4c345b91b7c5b5e38c4c74d00a83a5f9a0908249b5602045.svg) What are the coordinates of $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}= \\left\\{ \\mathbf{c}_1, \\mathbf{c}_2 \\right\\},$ where $\\mathbf{c}_1 = -\\mathbf{b}_2$ and $\\mathbf{c}_2 = -3\\mathbf{b}_1?$" :question/worked-solution "Since ![](images/f2/f2c4bc20db558b0e0498da6c9b8653f8e093a7b3a517a21ec07803cdf3debf98.svg) we can write $\\mathbf{x}$ in terms of the vectors $\\mathbf c_1$ and $\\mathbf c_2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5{b}_{1}-2{b}_{2} \\\\  & =-2{b}_{2}+5{b}_{1} \\\\  & =2(-{b}_{2})-\\frac{5}{3}(-3{b}_{1}) \\\\  & =2{c}_{1}-\\frac{5}{3}{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ![](images/f6/f6a8d41276d74ce726301f3cc83d87542d49115eb46cf0f836d20816cd660168.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119635 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119824 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "df96d21a-e1bc-5a9b-b528-d6e7b30d8451" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Three-Dimensional Vector in a Different Basis"}] :question/id #uuid "f07356b8-aeb3-50ac-b017-c370d87395d2" :question/math-academy-id "e-4828" :question/problem "Let $\\mathcal{B}= \\left\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2,\\mathbf{b}_3 \\right\\}$ be a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ and ![](images/19/191549b963b841cb97bbad9b6c65019d913cc148ed466d3c026cc27eccff1d8d.svg) What are the coordinates of $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}= \\left\\{ \\mathbf{c}_1, \\mathbf{c}_2, \\mathbf{c}_3 \\right\\},$ where $\\mathbf{c}_1 = -3\\mathbf{b}_2,$ $\\mathbf{c}_2=-\\mathbf{b}_3,$ and $\\mathbf{c}_3 = -4\\mathbf{b}_1?$" :question/worked-solution "Since ![](images/33/334e52d3fd172cadf353bd6b3d6f3fe66eb0c722320da14ce6fd11b66cdca79a.svg) we can write $\\mathbf{x}$ in terms of the vectors $\\mathbf c_1, \\mathbf c_2,$ and $\\mathbf c_3,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-2{b}_{1}-3{b}_{2}+5{b}_{3} \\\\  & =-3{b}_{2}+5{b}_{3}-2{b}_{1} \\\\  & =(-3{b}_{2})-5(-{b}_{3})+\\frac{1}{2}(-4{b}_{1}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}-5{c}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ![](images/9a/9a0eec63eca72981c016e4c504a49d249b6514e5e4f12e77e7d721282b7b24bb.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119636 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119825 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e568b6fc-88f0-592b-a24d-e3286cfa5043" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Vector in a Different Basis Given a Linear Relationship Between Two Bases"}] :question/id #uuid "91fac4cd-7097-5244-a385-9f3f76395912" :question/math-academy-id "e-4829" :question/problem "Let $\\mathcal{B}= \\left\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2 \\right\\}$ be a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and ![](images/e8/e8c67f642148caf84c01b7ee88fe125c4a5ae37ecae313d01d45c277de51d74b.svg) What are the coordinates of $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}= \\left\\{ \\mathbf{c}_1, \\mathbf{c}_2 \\right\\},$ where $\\mathbf{c}_1 = \\mathbf{b}_2-\\mathbf{b}_1$ and $\\mathbf{c}_2 = 2\\mathbf{b}_1 - 3\\mathbf{b}_2?$" :question/worked-solution "We are given that\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}={b}_{2}-{b}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}=2{b}_{1}-3{b}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the $\\mathbf{b}$'s in terms of $\\mathbf{c}$'s. Multiplying the first equation by $2$ and adding the equations, we get \n\n$$\n2\\mathbf{c}_1+\\mathbf{c}_2 = -\\mathbf{b}_2\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\mathbf{b}_2 = -2\\mathbf{c}_1-\\mathbf{c}_2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}_1 = \\left( -2\\mathbf{c}_1-\\mathbf{c}_2 \\right) - \\mathbf{b}_1\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\mathbf{b}_1 = -3\\mathbf{c}_1-\\mathbf{c}_2.\n$$\n\nSince ![](images/0e/0e283c9948eacf3e231eef3a3feb355c33f20c3dbfd89e97c530331758af34ad.svg) we can write the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ in terms of the vectors $\\mathbf c_1$ and $\\mathbf c_2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={b}_{1}-3{b}_{2} \\\\  & =(-3{c}_{1}-{c}_{2})-3(-2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}) \\\\  & =-3{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}+6{c}_{1}+3{c}_{2} \\\\  & =3{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ![](images/d8/d8d25ca46df4435c67e7c23e61851e2acd4826b0b2d99d9b54de8b6f2c37ee94.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247301 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119823 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29b27833-e930-5339-89b3-c5651d791dcd" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Two-Dimensional Vector in a Different Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24218213-a5eb-5590-9e7b-218c4d063827" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244629} {:answer/id #uuid "85fa3382-f478-524a-9e80-65cf1a73020c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245493} {:answer/id #uuid "98d0bad8-d4e1-5157-8dc4-cba2376d7a63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245667} {:answer/id #uuid "d505785e-f679-5234-ab57-c3a075e8844c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246216} {:answer/id #uuid "e94069e5-a01d-5639-8cd9-e141ddd7c241" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246382}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85fa3382-f478-524a-9e80-65cf1a73020c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245493} :answer-field/id #uuid "1eefc599-7ba2-5b15-95e6-94af3f1a1ca4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246599}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1a40e36f-1c5f-56d9-8cff-9a214e68eceb" :question/math-academy-id "q-112632" :question/problem "Let $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and $[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\},$ where ${b}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{2}$ and ${b}_{2}=-{c}_{1}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$, we can write $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\  & ={c}_{2}+2{c}_{1} \\\\  & =2(\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{2})-2(-{c}_{1}) \\\\  & =2{b}_{1}-2{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380636 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119824 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "df96d21a-e1bc-5a9b-b528-d6e7b30d8451" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Three-Dimensional Vector in a Different Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4eba5ccd-5c4e-5a36-a064-4ef635ea962c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380638} {:answer/id #uuid "153e965c-0ba4-51b3-b2cc-0690963e8754" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380639} {:answer/id #uuid "26addb96-9bcf-50c8-96e8-46e354758c47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380640} {:answer/id #uuid "3e6c74e4-e674-54ce-a79c-25df9fdacc3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380641} {:answer/id #uuid "97952375-bc23-52ad-9a79-64c3a8443c09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380642}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "153e965c-0ba4-51b3-b2cc-0690963e8754" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380639} :answer-field/id #uuid "76499bf7-e986-58f6-9967-cfbce16c554a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380637}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7c19afb3-436b-5bc9-8f6f-99c189134e6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-112812" :question/problem "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ be a basis of ${R}^{3}$ and $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}].$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}\\},$ where ${c}_{1}=4{b}_{3}$, ${c}_{2}=2{b}_{1}$ and ${c}_{3}=3{b}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]$, we can write $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4{b}_{1}-6{b}_{2}+8{b}_{3} \\\\  & =8{b}_{3}+4{b}_{1}-6{b}_{2} \\\\  & =2(4{b}_{3})+2(2{b}_{1})-2(3{b}_{2}) \\\\  & =2{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}-2{c}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432499 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119825 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e568b6fc-88f0-592b-a24d-e3286cfa5043" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Vector in a Different Basis Given a Linear Relationship Between Two Bases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06929580-b4ba-53af-ac69-8bc84360d71e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432469} {:answer/id #uuid "6599960a-b761-54ae-800a-4099e8dc6454" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432480} {:answer/id #uuid "7238f33a-029e-5920-a373-cb968bcc10aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432481} {:answer/id #uuid "7960b759-a049-517f-91ce-8f3d3d4ab81c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432482} {:answer/id #uuid "8d5905dd-9d56-563e-ab3a-014628903d88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432484}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6599960a-b761-54ae-800a-4099e8dc6454" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432480} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c066721-df4d-58dc-9748-1ef1119a9c18" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432494}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1f3bd7e4-e9ee-5ec1-b495-697d442c712e" :question/math-academy-id "q-113070" :question/problem "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\},$ where ${c}_{1}=2{b}_{1}+{b}_{2}$ and ${c}_{2}=-{b}_{1}?$" :question/worked-solution "We are given that\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}=2{b}_{1}+{b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2}=-{b}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the $b$'s in terms of $c$'s. From the second equation, we get \n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=-{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=2(-{c}_{2})+{b}_{2}\\,⟹\\,{b}_{2}={c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSince $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$, we can write the vector $x$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-{b}_{1}+3{b}_{2} \\\\  & =-(-{c}_{2})+3({c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}) \\\\  & ={c}_{2}+3{c}_{1}+6{c}_{2} \\\\  & =7{c}_{2}+3{c}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410058 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119825 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e568b6fc-88f0-592b-a24d-e3286cfa5043" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Vector in a Different Basis Given a Linear Relationship Between Two Bases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c013c9c-5216-521c-878e-e22df5716d0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410018} {:answer/id #uuid "7b46227a-c88c-5619-8cec-a1c99318b271" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410030} {:answer/id #uuid "94609691-7cfa-58f9-b339-110e2b9cb3d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410032} {:answer/id #uuid "b20f4407-028f-5333-8613-ef2137552c30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410039} {:answer/id #uuid "db1d9153-b78b-548a-ae9b-7d2f2545ca63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410044}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1c013c9c-5216-521c-878e-e22df5716d0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410018} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f810d6a-b71d-5aa6-a732-e9b3cdb4d3fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410052}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "07f1e9fd-ac52-515f-8ec2-7b10b57c07ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-113095" :question/problem "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\},$ where ${c}_{1}=2{b}_{1}-{b}_{2}$ and ${c}_{2}={b}_{2}-{b}_{1}?$" :question/worked-solution "We are given that\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}=2{b}_{1}-{b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2}=-{b}_{1}+{b}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the $b$'s in terms of $c$'s. Adding the equations, we get \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}={b}_{1}\\,⟹\\,{b}_{1}={c}_{1}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=2({c}_{1}+{c}_{2})-{b}_{2}\\,⟹\\,{b}_{2}={c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSince $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$, we can write the vector $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4{b}_{1}-2{b}_{2} \\\\  & =4({c}_{1}+{c}_{2})-2({c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}) \\\\  & =4{c}_{1}+4{c}_{2}-2{c}_{1}-4{c}_{2} \\\\  & =2{c}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424437 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119825 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e568b6fc-88f0-592b-a24d-e3286cfa5043" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Vector in a Different Basis Given a Linear Relationship Between Two Bases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11a1b0ee-a4a2-5576-8c66-7aaec2ee0e69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424424} {:answer/id #uuid "22b18ee1-002f-5c66-b260-413039c7fa38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424426} {:answer/id #uuid "44ad6232-afa5-5eee-acd4-ac7923c6c4c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424427} {:answer/id #uuid "54f9c040-8ec5-5672-a995-f18c78ec39cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424429} {:answer/id #uuid "d19d735a-e23a-5d22-b726-fc09edb321eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424433}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22b18ee1-002f-5c66-b260-413039c7fa38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424426} :answer-field/id #uuid "f715ad10-8843-5e87-a878-4728db817271" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424435}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "462a3ac8-3692-5123-988e-2869c674b5a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-113098" :question/problem "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\},$ where ${c}_{1}=-2{b}_{2}$ and ${c}_{2}=-2{b}_{1}+{b}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "We are given that\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}=-2{b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2}=-2{b}_{1}+{b}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the $b$'s in terms of $c$'s. From the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n{b}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{c}_{2}=-2{b}_{1}+(-\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{1})\\,⟹\\,{b}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{4}{c}_{1}-\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSince $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]$, we can write the vector $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-8{b}_{1}+6{b}_{2} \\\\  & =-8(-\\frac{1}{4}{c}_{1}-\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{2})+6(-\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{1}) \\\\  & =2{c}_{1}+4{c}_{2}-3{c}_{1} \\\\  & =-{c}_{1}+4{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120361 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119825 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e568b6fc-88f0-592b-a24d-e3286cfa5043" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Vector in a Different Basis Given a Linear Relationship Between Two Bases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b460d7d-05fc-5b15-8765-b5b270de1457" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432474} {:answer/id #uuid "4c342d58-92e7-533b-8f8a-f85d38cc91c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432475} {:answer/id #uuid "ab88d19a-3124-5017-a14e-eded936a6b24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ \\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432486} {:answer/id #uuid "ac17bbb1-ef71-5066-9361-5ab9de0c1d4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432487} {:answer/id #uuid "b8489fb5-7510-5290-99ff-82eff74dc7d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432488}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c342d58-92e7-533b-8f8a-f85d38cc91c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432475} :answer-field/id #uuid "02f00930-e0df-504b-a2ac-ea6475c9a01e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118280}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6baeb846-6cef-58be-ac89-933fb6011df4" :question/math-academy-id "q-34771" :question/problem "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\},$ where ${c}_{1}={b}_{1}+{b}_{2}$ and ${c}_{2}=-{b}_{1}?$" :question/worked-solution "We are given that\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}={b}_{1}+{b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2}=-{b}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the $b$'s in terms of $c$'s. From the second equation, we get \n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=-{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=(-{c}_{2})+{b}_{2}\\,⟹\\,{b}_{2}={c}_{1}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSince $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$, we can write the vector $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-3{b}_{1}+4{b}_{2} \\\\  & =-3(-{c}_{2})+4({c}_{1}+{c}_{2}) \\\\  & =3{c}_{2}+4{c}_{1}+4{c}_{2} \\\\  & =4{c}_{1}+7{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432500 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119823 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29b27833-e930-5339-89b3-c5651d791dcd" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Two-Dimensional Vector in a Different Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "198e300a-44d0-5694-88ed-2170ad2ca1e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432470} {:answer/id #uuid "404cd653-49d5-53d4-97a1-86fa615b47b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432472} {:answer/id #uuid "559920af-21be-5fab-b579-0d1866bc1809" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432478} {:answer/id #uuid "dd47cc6e-7faa-5406-9624-2956e3795db8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432490} {:answer/id #uuid "f4b29cca-110e-5949-a6ce-e19ab93bd9d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432492}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "198e300a-44d0-5694-88ed-2170ad2ca1e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432470} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d65567a-61e9-526a-b757-4f395c20b9a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432495}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2f3e71ae-af49-5472-af32-b57e5521039a" :question/math-academy-id "q-34773" :question/problem "Let $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and $[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\},$ where ${b}_{1}=4{c}_{2}$ and ${b}_{2}=-2{c}_{1}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]$, we can write $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-3{c}_{1}-5{c}_{2} \\\\  & =-5{c}_{2}-3{c}_{1} \\\\  & =-5({c}_{2})+3(-{c}_{1}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{4}(4{c}_{2})+\\frac{3}{2}(-2{c}_{1}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{4}{b}_{1}+\\frac{3}{2}{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120383 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119823 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29b27833-e930-5339-89b3-c5651d791dcd" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Two-Dimensional Vector in a Different Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1787f492-78b1-512d-8267-7681cdc274c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}\\